    <h1 class="case-title">How Niching Down to Weddings Built $30K in Bookings for a String Quartet</h1>

    <h2 class="case-section">The Challenge</h2>
    <p>A string quartet was booking a little of everything — weddings, corporate events, private parties — with a general-purpose brand that didn't clearly speak to any one of them.</p>
    <p>That's a harder position to book from than it looks. A generic "live music for any occasion" brand doesn't just fail to stand out — it competes against every category of musician and entertainer at once, instead of owning the one moment where a quartet is genuinely the obvious choice.</p>

    <h2 class="case-section">My Role</h2>
    <p>Over a 12-month engagement, I led booking strategy, brand positioning, and a full website rebuild — no social media component. The bookings themselves were mine to secure and mine to attribute.</p>

    <div class="case-cta">
      <p>Want to see how a decision-first strategy could work for your business?</p>
      <a href="./index.html#contact" class="case-cta-btn">Let's Talk &rarr;</a>
    </div>

    <h2 class="case-section">The Strategy</h2>
    <p>The core decision was to narrow, not broaden: reposition the quartet specifically around weddings, rather than continuing to market as an all-occasion ensemble.</p>
    <p>That decision shaped everything downstream. The website was rebuilt around wedding-specific language, imagery, and content — speaking directly to the couple standing in the middle of that decision, not a generic event planner. And rather than trying to reach couples cold, the strategy built referral partnerships with other wedding vendors — planners, venues, photographers — placing the quartet directly inside the vendor ecosystem couples were already consulting and trusting during active wedding planning.</p>
    <div class="case-pullquote">A referral from a trusted planner or venue meets a couple inside their actual decision context. A cold ad meets them nowhere in particular. That's the same principle behind the Context factor in my C.O.R.E framework — where someone is standing when they decide matters as much as what you say to them.</div>

    <hr class="case-rule">

    <h2 class="case-section">The Outcome</h2>
    <div class="hero-stat">
      <div class="hero-stat-num">$30K</div>
      <div class="hero-stat-label">In Booking Value Over 12 Months</div>
    </div>
    <div class="case-callout">
      <div class="case-callout-label">A Note on Attribution</div>
      <p>Because booking was handled directly, this figure reflects bookings personally secured during the engagement — not a modeled or estimated contribution.</p>
    </div>

    <hr class="case-rule">

    <h2 class="case-section">Key Takeaway</h2>
    <p>Trying to be right for everyone usually means being the obvious choice for no one. Narrowing to a single, well-defined occasion didn't shrink the opportunity — it sharpened it, and it made the quartet easy to refer, easy to recommend, and easy to book.</p>

    <div class="case-insight">
      <div class="case-insight-label">Key Takeaway</div>
      <p>&ldquo;A brand that fits one moment perfectly will out-book a brand that fits every moment vaguely.&rdquo;</p>
    </div>

